**How to read the table.** Each row is the stage where an attack starts, from S1 (a user engages with a phishing lure) to S6 (ransomware staged). The first two columns give the expected time until the incident ends in any way, in 4-hour steps and in hours. The next four give the chance it ends contained or catastrophic (data theft or ransomware), under the baseline and under an improved posture. The last column is the gain in containment, in percentage points.

**What the table tells you.** The later an attack is caught, the worse the odds: containment falls from 72.5% for an attack starting at S1 to 10.5% at S6 under the baseline. The improved posture lifts containment at every stage, by 24.8 to 40.5 points. All inputs are illustrative assumptions, so these figures show how the model behaves, not the risk to any real organization.

**In more detail**

**Catastrophic outcomes overtake containment at S3.** Under the baseline parameters, catastrophic probability rises from 38.1% at S2 to 52.9% at S3 (versus 47.1% containment) and reaches 89.5% by S6. This is a consequence of the hand-set parameters, and the [uncertainty analysis](robustness.qmd#parameter-uncertainty-dirichlet) attaches a 90% interval of 42.3%–63.5% to the S3 value. It is suggestive that identity and credential controls matter around this stage, but it does not show that perimeter defense alone is insufficient.

**The window shortens with depth.** Expected time to resolution falls from 14.2 hours at S1 to 4.2 hours at S6, a decline of 70%. This is the time to any resolution; it shrinks partly because fewer stages remain. The time scale rests on the assumed 4-hour step.

**Improvement from a hand-set matrix.** Moving from the baseline to the illustrative improved posture raises containment by 24.8 to 40.5 percentage points depending on the starting stage, with the largest uplifts at S3 and S4 (39.9 and 40.5 pp). Because all rows change together, this does not identify which stage's control matters most; see the [one-row-at-a-time table](robustness.qmd#one-row-at-a-time-control-sensitivity).

